{{template "base.gohtml"}}

{{define "title"}}AuthThingie 2 Setup{{end}}

{{define "content"}}
    <div id="ftue-0-container" class="at-page">
        <hgroup>
            <h1>Welcome to AuthThingie 2</h1>
            <h2>Step 1 of 2 - Your Environment and Network</h2>
        </hgroup>

        {{ if .Errors }}
            <div class="error-box at-error-list" id="ftue-error-box">
                <strong>We couldn't continue with these settings:</strong>
                <ul>
                    {{ range .Errors }}
                        <li>{{ . }}</li>
                    {{ end }}
                </ul>
            </div>
        {{ end }}

        <form action="/ftue/step0" method="post">
            <article>
                <header>
                    <strong>General Configuration</strong>
                </header>

                <label for="port-field">Port to listen on</label>
                <input type="text" name="port" id="port-field" required aria-label="Server Port" value="{{ .Port }}" />
                <small>This is the port AuthThingie itself binds to. If you're running in Docker, this is the port <em>inside</em> the container.</small>
            </article>

            <article>
                <header>
                    <strong>Configuration Path</strong>
                </header>

                <p>Where should the config file and the database live?</p>

                <details>
                    <summary>How do I pick?</summary>
                    <p>
                        Whatever you pick, it is <strong>highly recommended</strong> that it lives on a mounted volume so it survives restarts. If you keep
                        seeing this screen while running in Docker, an unmounted config directory is almost certainly why. I've tried to detect your
                        environment and pre-select a sane default, but you can always specify a fully custom path.
                    </p>
                </details>

                <fieldset id="ftue-config-path-choices">
                    <label for="slash-config-radio">
                        <input type="radio" id="slash-config-radio" name="config_file_preset" value="slashconfig" {{ if .DefaultSlashConfig }} checked {{ end }} />
                        <code>/config</code>
                        <small>A good choice if you're using Docker.</small>
                    </label>
                    <label for="cwd-radio">
                        <input type="radio" id="cwd-radio" name="config_file_preset" value="pwd" {{ if .DefaultPWD }} checked {{ end }} />
                        <code>{{ .PWD }}</code>
                        <small>The current working directory. A good choice if you're running outside of Docker.</small>
                    </label>
                    <label for="custom-radio">
                        <input type="radio" id="custom-radio" name="config_file_preset" value="custom" {{ if .DefaultCustom }} checked {{ end }} />
                        Custom paths
                        <small>Only pick this if you know exactly what you're doing.</small>
                    </label>

                    <div id="custom-path-fields">
                        <div class="grid">
                            <label for="config-file-path">
                                Config file path (including name)
                                <input type="text" name="config_path" id="config-file-path" value="{{ .CustomConfigPath }}" autocomplete="off" autocorrect="off" spellcheck="false" />
                            </label>
                            <label for="db-file-path">
                                Database file (including name)
                                <input type="text" name="db_path" id="db-file-path" value="{{ .CustomDBPath }}" autocomplete="off" autocorrect="off" spellcheck="false" />
                            </label>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </fieldset>
            </article>

            <article>
                <header>
                    <strong>Domain Configuration</strong>
                </header>

                <p>AuthThingie needs to know how it's served to the outside world so it can set cookies and redirects correctly.</p>

                <details>
                    <summary>What goes in these fields?</summary>
                    <p>
                        For <strong>Server Domain</strong>, use the apex domain everything is hosted on. If you have <code>foo.example.com</code> and
                        <code>bar.example.com</code>, then use <strong>example.com</strong>.
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        For <strong>Auth URL</strong>, use the full URL AuthThingie itself lives on. If AuthThingie is reachable at
                        <code>https://auth.example.com</code>, then use <strong>https://auth.example.com</strong>. I've tried to fill this in for you.
                    </p>
                </details>

                <div class="grid">
                    <div>
                        <label for="domain-field">Server Domain</label>
                        <input type="text" name="domain" id="domain-field" required aria-label="Server Domain" value="{{ .ServerDomain }}" autocomplete="off" autocorrect="off" spellcheck="off" />
                        <small>e.g. <code>example.com</code></small>
                    </div>
                    <div>
                        <label for="auth-url-field">Auth URL</label>
                        <input type="text" name="auth_url" id="auth-url-field" required aria-label="Auth URL Field" value="{{ .AuthURL }}" autocomplete="off" autocorrect="off" spellcheck="off" />
                        <small>e.g. <code>https://auth.example.com</code></small>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </article>

            <article>
                <header>
                    <strong>Trusted Proxy Configuration</strong>
                </header>

                <p>
                    AuthThingie needs to know where your reverse proxy lives so it knows which systems to trust. You can do this by
                    <strong>Docker labels</strong> (recommended) or by <strong>IP range</strong>. I've tried to detect both for you.
                </p>

                <h4>Docker Container Trust</h4>

                {{ if .DockerDetectionError }}
                    <p class="at-detection-status at-detection-failed">
                        I couldn't connect to the Docker daemon at <code>{{ .DockerEndpoint }}</code>: <code>{{ .DockerDetectionError }}</code>.
                       You can either fix that, or use network trust down below.
                    </p>
                    <input type="hidden" name="docker_detected" value="false" />
                {{ else }}
                    <input type="hidden" name="docker_endpoint" value="{{ .DockerEndpoint }}" />
                    <input type="hidden" name="docker_detected" value="true" />
                    <p class="at-detection-status at-detection-ok">
                        Connected to the Docker daemon at <code>{{ .DockerEndpoint }}</code> and looked for containers labelled
                        <code>auththingie2.trusted_proxy=true</code>.
                    </p>
                    {{ if .DetectedContainers }}
                        <input type="hidden" name="containers_found" value="true" />
                        <figure>
                            <table role="grid">
                                <thead>
                                    <tr>
                                        <th scope="col">ID</th>
                                        <th scope="col">Image</th>
                                        <th scope="col">Names</th>
                                    </tr>
                                </thead>
                                <tbody>
                                    {{ range .DetectedContainers }}
                                        <tr>
                                            <td><code>{{ .ShortID }}</code></td>
                                            <td>{{ .Image }}</td>
                                            <td>{{ .AllNames }}</td>
                                        </tr>
                                    {{ end }}
                                </tbody>
                            </table>
                        </figure>
                        <p>If that looks right, you're golden! You can skip the IP based trust below entirely.</p>
                    {{ else }}
                        <input type="hidden" name="containers_found" value="false" />
                        <p class="at-empty">
                            No labelled containers found. Add the label <code>auththingie2.trusted_proxy=true</code> to your reverse proxy and reload this
                            page, or use network based trust below.
                        </p>
                    {{ end }}
                {{ end }}

                <details>
                    <summary>I'm using a different Docker endpoint</summary>
                    <p>
                        I looked for the Docker API at <code>{{ .DockerEndpoint }}</code>. If that's wrong, set the environment variable
                        <code>SETUP_DOCKER_ENDPOINT</code> to the right location and reload this page.
                    </p>
                </details>

                <h4>Network Based Trust</h4>

                <p>
                    If Docker detection doesn't work for you, you can trust an entire IP range instead. Be <strong>damn</strong> sure you only trust private
                    ranges you control, and keep them as narrow as possible. Here's what I found on your running network interfaces.
                </p>

                {{ if .CandidateIPRanges }}
                    <figure>
                        <table role="grid">
                            <thead>
                                <tr>
                                    <th scope="col">Trust</th>
                                    <th scope="col">Interface Name</th>
                                    <th scope="col">Assigned IP</th>
                                    <th scope="col">IP Range</th>
                                </tr>
                            </thead>
                            <tbody>
                                {{ range .CandidateIPRanges }}
                                    <tr>
                                        <td><input type="checkbox" name="trusted_networks" value="{{ .Network }}" {{ if index $.SelectedNetworks .Network.String }}checked{{ end }} aria-label="Trust {{ .Network }} on {{ .InterfaceName }}" /></td>
                                        <td>{{ .InterfaceName }}</td>
                                        <td><code>{{ .LocalIP }}</code></td>
                                        <td><code>{{ .Network }}</code></td>
                                    </tr>
                                {{ end }}
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                    </figure>
                {{ else }}
                    <p class="at-empty">I couldn't find any private network ranges worth suggesting. You can still add one by hand below.</p>
                {{ end }}

                <label for="custom-trusted-network">Custom trusted network</label>
                <input type="text" name="custom_trusted_network" id="custom-trusted-network" value="{{ .CustomTrustedNetwork }}" placeholder="192.168.1.0/24" aria-label="Custom Trusted Network" autocomplete="off" autocorrect="off" spellcheck="false" />
                <small>A single IP works too. Leave this blank if you're using Docker labels or picked a range above.</small>
            </article>

            <button type="submit" class="contrast">Continue</button>
        </form>
    </div>

    <script src="/static/js/ftue.js"></script>
    <script src="/static/js/ftue0.js"></script>
{{end}}
